Also reproduced from first principles: 7161/6600/5598/1002, outer D1 `225` raw with per-offset ranks `41,38,33,25,19,11,6,3` = 176, h3 66→21, K2c 561→106 low-q, the 7 h2-D1 rows at e-powers 291–295, the design's identities `c₂,₉=3u, c₃,₈=−3v, c₇,₄=c−3u²v, c₁₀,₁=−cv`, and `[x¹⁴⁵y¹⁷]J = 1764·A3₉₈,₀`. Singular replays: dim −1, basis 1, controls pass. Runtime 33 s / 26 MB RSS versus the charged engine's ~2 h / 279 MB — because after the D2 preblock `KF ≡ K2³` and `KG ≡ K2² + tK_B1K2` exactly through t-power 21.

**Two things you should know.**

The `(16,12)` calibration is a **GAP**, not a pass: printed pp. 207–209 extract as prose, but every display equation defining Moh's 17-coefficient p.208 ansatz is missing from the PDF text layer, and the charged reports give only its counts. I did not reconstruct it.

The requested control has a false premise: **no two-point automorphism of degrees ≥ 6 exists** (Jung–van der Kulk forces a common leading linear form above degree 1) — a two-point pair with J=1 at those degrees *is* the counterexample under test. I built the nearest real object instead, a tame `(6,36)` automorphism with its single point inside the chart; its entire band family collapses to the one slot `[t⁴⁰w⁰]=1`, and a perturbed weight breaks it.

Two relaxation controls also pin the mechanism: dropping the Jacobian band leaves δ=2 alive at 945, dropping the pole rows leaves it at 915 — the death is their interaction, and the four-stage gap between branches is that `t = s²` gives δ=5/2 a nonzero G-pole row only at even local powers.

The chart-wide conclusion still rests on the same necessity and branch-exhaustiveness gates; re-implementation removes implementation fidelity as a doubt, nothing more.
